

titre$ TAXA IN   THE   COLLYBIA   DRYOPHILA  COMPLEX
com$ Adapt GF sept 2020

1,0$ source Rytas J. Vilgalys :A Biosystematic Study of Col/ybia dryophila ( Basidiomycetes: Tricholornataceae)  1982
1,0$


1,0 $ Portion of basidiocarp staining green in aqueous alkaline solution $ C. alkalivirens and allies(·see Halling, 1981)
1,2 $ Basidiocarp not staining green in alkaline solution
2,3 $ Occuring exclusively on soil in early spring
2,5 $ Occuring on leaf litter, rotted wood, or mulch (if on soil, then at least sorne sporocarps on litter) through- out fruiting season
3,0 $ Stipe with tawny strigosity at base $ C. earleae
3,4 $ Stipe without tawny strigosity at base
4,0 $ Occuring in open areas (lawns, flower beds, etc.), lamellae close (not crowded), pileus tawny $ C. agricola
4,0 $ Occuring in woods, lamellae very crowded, pileus pale isabelline $ C. virginiana
5,0 $ Pileus and stipe deep-reddish brown when young, with pileus later fading, basidiocarps densely cespitose on conifer wood $ C. acervata
5,6 $ Stipe light colored when young, darker only with age,  basidiocarps never densely cespitose on conifer wood  or, if cespitose, thenon another substrate
6,0 $ Stipe covered by a dense whitish vesture over entire  length, basidiocarp tinted rufescent and staining rufescent in all specimens, on leaves in oak woods, July-August $ C. kaufmanii  Halling, in ed.
6,7 $ Stipe glabrous with vesture only at base, basidiocarp without rufescent hues, only staining rufescentin mature specimens
7,0 $ Young basidiocarp sulphur-yellow, with yellow color saon fading, stipe with pinkish rhizomorphps at base, lamellae yellow (rarely white) when fresh and saon fading to white, cespitose in small clusters in early spring or late fall $ C. dryophila var. funicularis
7,8 $ Young basidiocarp tawny or darker, never yellow,  rhizomorphs never pink, lamellae white, gregarious to cespitose throughout fruiting season
8,0 $ Lamellae close, not crowàed, young pileus mummy-brown to seal-brown when fresh, lacking ochraceous colors, in small clusters on wood mulch and soil in early spring.. $ C. sp. nov. (RV 139)
8,0 $ Lamellae crowded, pileus tawny-ochraceous to tawny when fresh, gregarious to cespitose on leaf litter, rotted wood or mulch (with occasional basidiocarps on soil) $ C. dryophila var. dryophila
